# Private config source pointer (git-ignored).
#
# The public repo ships only committed templates for the operational data
# (etc/deploy.conf, etc/machines.ini, etc/reuter.ini, etc/team.ini, etc/hosts,
# etc/host-hardening.php). The real values live in a separate,
# access-controlled private repository — a small git repo whose tracked files
# are `deploy.conf`, `machines.ini`, `reuter.ini`, `team.ini`, `hosts` and
# `host-hardening.php` (mirroring this repo's etc/). This file tells simox's
# `bin/fetch-private-data` where to find that repo.
#
# Copy this file to `.private-source` (untracked, machine-specific) and set
# the one retrieval mechanism — a git URL (+ optional ref), fetched on demand
# into var/private-data (git-ignored, like the rest of /var/) and copied from
# there into etc/ as real files:
#
# PRIVATE_DATA_GIT=git@example.com:team/simox-private-config.git
# PRIVATE_DATA_REF=main
#
# The private repo's committed content is the single source of truth: no
# machine runs on a loose, uncommitted copy. `reuter.ini` is the only file
# that ever ships to a prod host (whole, via the framework's
# DEPLOY_PRIVATE_FILES key); `deploy.conf` is deploy-machine-only (its values
# travel as replayed environment), and `machines.ini`, `team.ini`, `hosts` and
# `host-hardening.php` stay dev/deploy-time and never leave the deploy
# machine. A `.private-source` that sets no PRIVATE_DATA_GIT is an error, not
# a silent skip; an absent `.private-source` is a no-op (prod has no git and
# no pointer — it receives reuter.ini from the deploy machine's deploy step).
#
# This file never discloses the private repo's identity in the public history
# (it is git-ignored). Access control on the private repo is the real security
# boundary — see doc/system/private-config.md.
